Introduction
Most machine learning methods that reach MetaTrader arrive with some version of the same bill attached. Training is expensive, so you retrain on a cadence and hope nothing important happened in between. The model is a block of floating point weights, so reproducing a result on another machine means reproducing a sequence of floating point operations in the same order. And when the model finally prints a number, you have no idea what that number would look like if the model knew nothing at all.
Hyperdimensional Computing, set out by Pentti Kanerva in 2009 and also known as Vector Symbolic Architecture, addresses all three at once, by giving up something most would not think they could: no single weight in the model means anything. Every concept, feature name and quantity is one very long random vector, and there are exactly three operations.
Bind
is XOR,
bundle
is an element-wise majority,
permute
is a cyclic shift. That is the whole algebra.
What you get in exchange is worth having in a trading terminal specifically. Learning is an integer add into an accumulator, so a model absorbs one more labeled row in a few hundred microseconds rather than waiting for a refit. The classifier core is integer and bitwise end to end, so a reference implementation of that core does not produce a similar model, it produces the same model. And because two unrelated hypervectors have a known distance distribution, the noise floor a reading is measured against is a closed-form expression rather than a permutation run that costs seconds per bar.
Each of those three has a boundary, and this article marks the boundary where it arises. A row carries a label that reads five bars ahead, so the newest row that can be folded in sits five bars behind the close. Feature engineering upstream of the encoder is ordinary double arithmetic, and only what happens from the quantizer onwards is exact. And the closed-form band describes unrelated prototypes, which the prototypes of a trained model are not.
This article builds that library. Nothing of the kind exists in the MQL5 catalog, so every part is from scratch: the algebra, the codebook, the classifier, and the tests that judge them. It is written for a developer who wants a classifier they can reason about completely, and it is honest about what the finished library finds in price data, which is very little. The last two sections are about measuring that, because a method that cannot be refuted is not a method.
What the library does, before any theory
The finished library builds a two-class model from six causal features on a bar, and reports a signed decision variable called the margin. Here is what its market scan prints on XAUUSDm D1 over 3000 bars, comparing a model kept up to date one bar at a time against the same model refitted from scratch on a cadence:
=== 7. online updating against a batch refit ===
      670 rows scored: online 0.4866, batch-refit 0.5060, they agree 38.1%
      cost: 670 online updates 226 ms total (337 us each), 27 batch refits 9571 ms total
=== 8. cost ===
      one full fit over 1674 rows: 425 ms (min of 3)
      which is 254 us per row, and the encoding is nearly all of it
The cost line is worth reading twice. Six hundred and seventy online updates took 226 milliseconds, so one update costs about 337 microseconds. Twenty-seven full refits over the same span took 9.6 seconds, so one refit of that span costs about 354 milliseconds. They differ by three orders of magnitude, but they are not the same unit of work: the update folds in one row, the refit re-encodes the whole span. Per row the full fit costs 254 microseconds against the update's 337, so nothing here says the accumulator is a faster way to encode a row. What it buys is that the history never has to be encoded again. Nor is the gap bought with an approximation: with the perceptron corrections switched off, so the comparison isolates the accumulator, the test suite asserts that folding one row in incrementally reproduces the full refit to the bit, worst error 0 bits. The accuracy figures above are a separate question, and the section on the facade answers it.
The scan also reports what the model is worth as a predictor, which is nothing. Its hold-out edge over the majority class is negative, negative in all six walk-forward folds, and comfortably inside two separate nulls. Both results matter and neither cancels the other.
Why the dimension is the whole idea
Start with the property everything else rests on. Take two random vectors of D bits and count the positions where they differ. That count is Binomial(D, 1/2), so it has mean D/2 and standard deviation sqrt(D)/2. Now express it as a similarity in the range from minus one to one:
sim =
1
-
2
* hamming / D
The mean becomes 0 and the standard deviation becomes
1 / sqrt(D)
. At D = 256 that is 0.0625, loose enough that about one unrelated pair in twenty looks more than 12 percent alike. At D = 4096 it is 0.0156. Only the dimension changed, and the consequence is that unrelated concepts stop colliding. Figure 1 draws the distribution at three dimensions using the library's own generator.
Fig. 1. The same experiment at three dimensions. The dotted lines mark one standard deviation and the dashed lines three. The distribution does not change shape, it only narrows, and that narrowing is what lets many facts share one vector
The library measures this rather than assuming it. On 400 pairs at D = 2048 the mean distance comes out 1025.54 against a predicted 1024.0, and the standard deviation 22.15 against a predicted 22.63. That is the whole justification for a noise band, and why it costs nothing to draw.
Because the distance is the only measurement the algebra makes, it is kept as an integer everywhere and converted to a float only for display. The features that feed the algebra are a separate matter and they are doubles; the exactness begins at the quantizer. The primitive is a straight population count over the XOR:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Integer bit distance: the primitive every similarity is built on |
//|                                                                  |
//|  SWAR and not the table: over three runs at D=2048, SWAR 0.057   |
//|  to 0.229 us against the table's 0.117 to 0.398. The 64 KB       |
//|  table misses L1, and it is kept as the independent check.       |
