The SWAR trick in
HDCPopSwar
adds bits in parallel inside a 64-bit register. A 65536-entry lookup table is also in the file, faster on paper and slower in practice because 64 KB does not stay in L1. It stays as an independent check on the SWAR version, and the test asserts the two agree on 5000 random words.
One constraint is worth stating plainly because it is enforced in code.
D must be a multiple of 64.
A ragged final word carries unused bits, and a population count cannot know they are not data, so every similarity would be wrong by an amount that depends on the seed.
CHDCVector::Init
refuses any other dimension, and the test checks that 1000 is refused and 1024 accepted.
Bind, bundle and permute
Three operations, each with one job.
Bind
associates two things and produces something unrelated to both. It is XOR, it is its own inverse, and it preserves every distance, which is the property that makes the rest of the algebra work:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| bind: XOR. Self inverse, and it preserves every distance         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
