The tie vector is what keeps that correlation from being systematic, and because it is drawn from the same seeded stream as everything else, the coin flip it stands in for is stable across runs and across languages. Figure 2 measures what each operation does.
Fig. 2. Every bar is one measurement at D = 4096, and the shaded strip is three standard deviations of the noise floor. Only two comparisons leave it: unbinding recovers the input exactly, and a bundle stays visibly close to its own members
