That closed form is only half the answer. What a caller wants is not the similarity but whether a member can still be picked out of a codebook, and that depends on the competition. The nearest of n unrelated distractors sits about sqrt(2 ln n) standard deviations from the mean, which for a 256-entry codebook is 3.33, so recall should hold while the member's signal clears 3.33 sd and break shortly after. Measured at D = 2048, recall is a perfect 1.000 down to 6.5 sd, 0.974 at 4.6 sd, and 0.814 at 3.2 sd. The theory said where it would break and it broke there.
That result bounds one component rather than describing the finished model. It is a statement about a plain majority bundle probed against a codebook, and the classifier does something else: it bundles six bound levels per bar, accumulates weighted rows into two classes, thresholds the result and then corrects it with perceptron passes. The capacity curve tells you when a bundle stops being readable. It does not predict the accuracy of the model built on top of it.
Fig. 3. The left panel is the closed form with measurements on top of it. The right panel converts the same curve into standard deviations, and the dashed line at 3.33 is where the nearest of 256 distractors sits, so recall breaks where the blue curve reaches it
The codebook: names, and quantities
Everything in the algebra is a random vector, but a feature value cannot be. Two names such as "ret1" and "maDist" have no order, so independent random vectors suit them. A quantity is different: a bar whose one-bar return sits at 1.9 standard deviations and a bar at 2.0 must come out similar, or the encoding throws away the structure it exists to capture.
The answer is a
level ladder
. Level 0 is a random vector, and each step up flips a fresh slice of one shared random permutation of the bit positions, the slices disjoint. Because they are disjoint, the bits separating level i from level j are exactly the bits flipped between them, so the distance between any two levels equals the difference of their cuts, with no approximation. Spend D/2 flips across the ladder and the extremes land exactly orthogonal.
