The cut function is one line of integer arithmetic,
cut(k) = k * (D/2) / (levels - 1)
, and it is shared with the reference implementation so that both sides place their flips identically. Figure 4 shows the result: not close to the closed form, equal to it.
Fig. 4. Sixteen levels at D = 2048. The measured points sit on the closed form rather than near it, and the matrix on the right is the same statement for every pair at once
The test suite checks all 256 pairs of a 16-level ladder and reports a worst error of
0 bits
, with the extremes at 1024 of 2048. That exactness is not decoration: it means a ladder can be regenerated anywhere from a seed and a count, which is what makes the cross-check an equality.
One thing about the codebook is easy to break by accident:
registration order is part of the contract
. Symbols and ladders are drawn from a single stream, so the order in which they are requested decides which bits each one gets. The facade and the reference implementation register the same names in the same sequence; change the order on one side only and every vector in the codebook changes.
Encoding a bar, and a window of bars
Six features per bar, all causal and all scale free. Scale free matters because a level ladder quantizes against a fixed range, and a raw price range on gold has nothing to do with one on EURUSD:
