The distinction between the accumulator and a bundle is easy to miss, and worth stating precisely because the obvious reading of it is wrong. A majority bundle saturates: at 63 members its similarity to any one of them is about 0.10, and by 501 it is 1.3 standard deviations above noise, barely visible. The accumulator does not escape that. Thresholded with every example weighted one it
is
the majority bundle, bit for bit, with the same ceiling. What it keeps that a bundle throws away is the weights. Measured at 501 examples: add one ten times rather than once and the prototype recovers it at a similarity of 0.3242, where a plain majority bundle of the same 501 gives 0.0283. The cause of that gap is the weight and not the accumulator, which is how the library's own test states it. The accumulator is what makes a weight expressible at all, which is what the perceptron corrections need. The sum is held in
int
, so its range is finite rather than unbounded: at weight one it would take on the order of two billion examples to matter, but it is a bound and not the absence of one. Only the query-time prototype is thresholded, and it is rebuilt whenever the model changes.
