That gap is not the full extent of the dependence between the two sides. Four of the six features are trailing estimates: a 100-bar standard deviation, a 14-bar ATR, a 20-bar average. Two rows a hundred bars apart are computed from overlapping windows of the same bars, so their features stay correlated long after their bars and their labels have stopped overlapping. Widening the gap to cover that would cost a hundred rows of a 1674-row sample and would still leave the correlation, because the windows are nested rather than adjacent. So the gap removes the sharing a naive cut creates in the bars and in the labels, and the features keep a slower dependence that no gap of this shape removes. The hold-out numbers later should be read with that in mind.
