What that establishes, and what it does not, is worth separating. The Python side rebuilds the model from the exported file, so the two implementations are compared on identical inputs: the same bars, feature values and labels. That proves determinism and an exact match between two codebooks, two encoders and two classifiers, which is stronger than any tolerance. It does not prove the chain reproduces from raw quotes, because the feature doubles arrive from the export rather than being recomputed independently in Python. The quantizer bounds are recomputed and match to zero, but a sort and a trim are exact operations on doubles already given. And none of it bears on whether the features are the right features. A bit-exact cross-check tells you the implementation is the one you specified; it says nothing about whether what you specified was worth running.
Two of those checks are controls rather than confirmations, and they are the ones that make the rest worth reading. A planted, genuinely separable feature is learned and transfers out of sample at 1.000. The same pipeline given randomly assigned labels scores 0.450, which is chance. A test suite that only ever demonstrates success cannot tell you whether the thing works.
What real markets say
The market scan runs the finished library over 3000 daily bars of gold and then tries to refute whatever it finds. The sample is 1674 labeled rows, 965 up and 709 down, so guessing the larger class scores 0.5765. The baseline is recomputed on whichever slice is being scored rather than carried over, which matters because a hold-out taken from the newest rows can have a very different class mix from the sample as a whole.
One thing the scan does not do should be named before any of its numbers are read. Everything it reports is classification accuracy against a majority baseline. There is no profit and loss anywhere in this library or this article, and no drawdown, turnover, spread, slippage or position sizing either. A label is the sign of a five-bar forward return that cleared a threshold, not a trade. A model can classify a label well and still lose money, and it can classify no better than chance and still sit inside a profitable system through sizing or timing. What follows is a statement about labels.
