Then the two nulls. Shuffling the labels in blocks of ten rows destroys the link between features and labels while leaving the runs that overlapping horizons create intact, and that null produces edges averaging -0.0749. Generating a matched random walk with the same bar count and the same per-bar volatility, and running it through the identical feature path, gives edges averaging -0.0386. The real edge sits at p = 0.76 against the first and p = 0.95 against the second.
Both of those are rank positions in twenty draws, the default
InpNulls
, and twenty is coarse: the finest value such a rank can take is one in twenty-one, so neither figure is a tail estimate. What they support is the weak reading, that the real edge sits in the body of each null distribution rather than out at an end of it, and for a result this far from significance that is all the nulls are being asked to do. Anyone who wants a p-value worth quoting should raise InpNulls, at a cost of one purged fit per draw.
Neither null is a model of the market either, and the second is deliberately crude. The walk is Gaussian with a constant per-bar sigma, so it matches bar count and volatility and nothing else: no fat tails, no volatility clustering, no gaps, no session structure, no asymmetry between a rise and a fall. The block shuffle has the opposite limitation: blocks of ten rows keep the short runs that overlapping horizons create, but moving the blocks breaks slow class drift and any link between the label mix and the volatility regime. Both destroy more structure than a conservative null would, which sets a bar that is, if anything, low. The real edge does not clear it.
Notice that both nulls are also negative. That is structural rather than a signal in disguise. A purged, ordered split combined with class imbalance produces a hold-out baseline that is genuinely hard to beat, so reading a negative edge as a contrarian indicator would be reading the split rather than the market. This is why the null has to be run alongside the finding.
